+#
+# system.cfg.backup is the record of the last system.cfg that was known to be
+# good (D-CLOUD-078, D-CLOUD-079). It is written from a file that has just
+# been checked -- at boot once verify has run, and at shutdown -- and never
+# from one that has not: an empty or truncated file is exactly what the
+# record exists to survive, and copying it over the record is how the RG SP
+# lost its settings (#102: a truncated system.cfg was backed up at the next
+# clean shutdown, and the boot after that found no good copy and seeded the
+# image defaults). Written to a temporary name beside it and renamed, so a
+# power cut mid-copy leaves the previous record and never half of one.
+#
+# verify, at boot, tries the live file, then the record, then the defaults,
+# in that order, and says in the system log which one it took. The image
+# defaults are the last resort, not the first fix.
+#
+# The file name is deliberately the one this script has always used, so an
+# upgraded device has one record at rest and nothing to migrate.
 
