+[Manager]
+# A kernel that has stopped scheduling is reset by the SoC's watchdog rather
+# than by somebody holding the power button -- which is what truncated two
+# config files on the RG SP (fork #102, #104; D-SYS-002). PID 1 pings it every
+# 7.5 s. 15 s because the Allwinner watchdog counts to 16 at most; a longer
+# figure is refused by the driver and systemd would then run with no watchdog
+# at all. A device without a watchdog device logs one line and carries on.
+RuntimeWatchdogSec=15s
+# Off on purpose. The same 16 s ceiling would have the shutdown watchdog
+# reset a device in the middle of a slow sync to the card, which is the very
+# corruption this is meant to prevent; systemd disarms the runtime watchdog
+# when it hands over to shutdown, so a hung shutdown behaves as it does today.
+RebootWatchdogSec=off
